tocol stuff worked well and  certificates were only being issued for  actual um you know service owners of  gmail  this shouldn't be needed but  it's responsible for catching a large  and very significant ssl man in the  middle attack that happened  in august 2011. um i think  the the story here is deserving of its  own 40-minute tech talk  which you know i obviously don't have  but i encourage you to search diginotar  and ssl if you're not familiar  with the specifics of this but the short  version is that  a certificate authority who actually  issues certificates was compromised  attackers were able to issue their own  certificates for  google services and because we have this  certificate pending built into chrome  this was a discrepancy to our white list  of what we'd expect  we got some warnings and then  investigated further and realized that  many users predominantly located in iran  were  vulnerable to man-in-the-middle attacks  so totally not realizing that even  though  it looked like they were accessing gmail  and other services over ssl  there was you know they had no security  or privacy in this connection so  certificate pinning  is cool another feature is  hsts which is the abbreviation for http  strict  transport security so this is  allowing services that really only want  their users to  access them over ssl to actually enforce  this at the browser level  there have been a number of of attacks  and ways to  downgrade users to  accessing a site over http even if it is  offered over https  and this is a way to give services some  power over how  users can access them there's a very  very  so how this works is that you know you  access  a website you access gmail or twitter  or facebook and if you just type in  you know twitter.com your browser is  going to resolve what protocol you want  to access it over  depending on the browser it may default  to http it may default to https  but then the service can actually  respond with this header and say  look browser you should only ever talk  to me over ssl  there's a really small window of  during that first response where  the user may actually access it over  http  um and this is where chrome actually  comes with some  whitelist another hard code a little bit  of a hack but  we white list services any service that  wants to elect into it so that  if you ever access these sites over in  chrome  you'll always access them over ssl  independent of any of these kind of  downgrade attacks that we've seen and  this is not a comprehensive  logo mashup but these are probably some  of the ones people are  maybe most familiar with so  um time wise i'm doing crap  but closing thoughts  so browser security matters i really  believe it should be a factor in the  software you choose  and i'm biased but you know what i would  recommend i think we do some cool things  to combat  current security threats and this was  sort of a probably appropriate  and high level more high level than um  you know what i would have liked to i  think there's a lot of interesting  uh technology and engineering behind  some of these features  uh if you're interested in them  or in any of this we have design docs  and a lot more information available  publicly  otherwise i'm i'm here normally i work  from  our main headquarters in mountain view  california but  i'm happy to chat during the conference  and otherwise  answer emails and thank you very much
 

